-- Q755: IT Infrastructure and Architecture [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Each microservice runs independently with isolated failure boundaries, preventing one service''s crash from cascading to others","All microservices share a single database, so the payment crash would also corrupt catalog and authentication data","Microservices cannot communicate with each other, which is why the other services were unaffected by the crash","The monolithic architecture would have handled the crash better because all components share memory and recover together"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under NIST SP 800-204 Section 2, microservices architecture decomposes applications into independently deployable services with isolated failure boundaries. Each service runs in its own process, so one service crashing does not cascade to others — unlike a monolith where a single failure can bring down the entire application.
Wrong (B): Microservices typically own their own data stores rather than sharing a single database, so a payment service crash would not corrupt other services'' data.
Wrong (C): Microservices do communicate via APIs and message queues; isolation comes from independent processes, not lack of communication.
Wrong (D): Monolithic architectures are more vulnerable to cascading failures because all components share a single process space.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 755;

-- Q758: IT Infrastructure and Architecture [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Managing and provisioning infrastructure through machine-readable configuration files rather than manual setup","Using spreadsheets to document server configurations and track changes over time","Writing custom firmware for network hardware to automate routing and switching functions","Coding applications that run directly on bare-metal servers without virtualization layers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under NIST SP 800-128 Section 2, Infrastructure as Code (IaC) manages and provisions computing infrastructure through machine-readable definition files such as Terraform or CloudFormation templates. This enables version control, repeatability, and automated deployments across environments.
Wrong (B): Spreadsheets are manual documentation tools that lack automated provisioning, version control, and the repeatability that defines IaC.
Wrong (C): Custom firmware development addresses hardware-level programming, not the declarative infrastructure provisioning that IaC encompasses.
Wrong (D): Bare-metal application development refers to running software without virtualization, not to managing infrastructure through code-based configuration.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 758;

-- Q760: ERP and Accounting Information Systems [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A standalone accounting application focused solely on general ledger management functions","A point-of-sale system designed primarily for processing retail sales transactions","A spreadsheet-based system used for manual financial consolidation across departments","An integrated suite of modules sharing a common database across all business functions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under COBIT 2019 Section BAI03, an ERP system integrates multiple business functions — finance, HR, procurement, production, and sales — into a single platform with a shared database. This eliminates data silos and enables real-time cross-departmental visibility.
Wrong (A): A standalone general ledger handles only financial transactions without cross-functional integration for HR, procurement, or production.
Wrong (B): A point-of-sale system processes retail transactions at checkout but does not integrate back-office functions like HR or procurement.
Wrong (C): Spreadsheet-based consolidation is manual and error-prone, lacking the automated real-time integration that defines an ERP system.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 760;

-- Q772: ERP and Accounting Information Systems [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Role-based access controls with a documented segregation matrix showing AP entry and payment approval assigned to separate users","A company policy memo stating that employees should not approve their own payment requests without exception","Super-user accounts shared among the accounting team with a verbal agreement to maintain segregation","An ERP configuration that logs all transactions but allows any authenticated user to perform any function"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): SOX Section 404 requires management to assess internal controls over financial reporting. Role-based access controls with a documented SoD matrix provide system-enforced preventive controls that auditors can test directly, offering the strongest evidence of control effectiveness.
Wrong (B): A policy memo establishes management intent but lacks system enforcement — users could violate the policy without detection.
Wrong (C): Shared super-user accounts eliminate individual accountability and make SoD enforcement impossible, directly contradicting control objectives.
Wrong (D): Logging provides detective controls after the fact but does not prevent unauthorized transactions from occurring.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 772;

-- Q773: ERP and Accounting Information Systems [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Manually recalculate depreciation for all 4,200 assets to ensure complete verification coverage","Review ERP depreciation settings against accounting policy, then test a stratified sample of both asset types","Accept the ERP output without testing because automated system calculations are inherently reliable","Test only equipment assets acquired in the current year because those are most likely to contain errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
