-- Q802: Security and Control Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["COBIT 2019 — IT governance and management framework by ISACA","ISO 27001 — information security management system standard","ITIL v4 — IT service management best practices framework","NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) — cybersecurity risk management"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The NIST CSF organizes cybersecurity activities into five core functions — Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover — providing a voluntary, risk-based structure specifically for managing cybersecurity risk (NIST CSF Section 2). It was published by NIST to help organizations structure cybersecurity programs.
Wrong (A): COBIT 2019 is an IT governance and management framework published by ISACA covering the full IT function, not a NIST cybersecurity-specific framework.
Wrong (B): ISO 27001 is an information security management system standard focused on establishing and certifying an ISMS, not specifically a cybersecurity risk framework by NIST.
Wrong (C): ITIL v4 focuses on IT service management practices, not on cybersecurity risk identification and response.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 802;

-- Q804: Security and Control Frameworks [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Provide Stakeholder Value, Holistic Approach, Dynamic Governance System","Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability (the CIA triad)","Plan, Do, Check, Act (the Deming cycle)","Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover (NIST CSF functions)"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): COBIT 2019 is built on six governance system principles: Provide Stakeholder Value, Holistic Approach, Dynamic Governance System, Governance Distinct from Management, Tailored to Enterprise Needs, and End-to-End Governance System (COBIT 2019 Section 3). These guide IT governance design.
Wrong (B): The CIA triad defines information security objectives, not IT governance principles — it is a foundational security concept, not a COBIT principle.
Wrong (C): Plan-Do-Check-Act is the Deming continuous improvement cycle used in quality management and ISO standards, not a COBIT governance principle.
Wrong (D): Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover are the five core functions of the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, not COBIT 2019 principles.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 804;

-- Q806: Security and Control Frameworks [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are competing frameworks that cannot be implemented together within one organization","COBIT focuses on IT governance while NIST CSF focuses on cybersecurity risk management; they complement each other","NIST CSF is a subset of COBIT and is fully contained within its governance domains","COBIT has replaced NIST CSF as the current industry standard for cybersecurity programs"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under COBIT 2019 Section EDM01, COBIT provides comprehensive IT governance and management while NIST CSF specifically addresses cybersecurity risk through its five core functions. Organizations often implement both for complete IT governance and cybersecurity coverage, as they address different but complementary concerns.
Wrong (A): The frameworks are complementary, not competing — many organizations successfully deploy both simultaneously for broader coverage.
Wrong (C): NIST CSF is an independent framework published by a different body (NIST vs. ISACA) and is not contained within COBIT.
Wrong (D): COBIT has not replaced NIST CSF; both remain actively maintained and widely adopted for their distinct purposes.$EXPL$
